            'body_en' => "The name means \"arch of the garden\", and that is what it was: a royal paradise — the Persian word that gave us the English one — laid out around a spring at the foot of the Zagros, on the Silk Road five kilometres from Kermanshah, where the Sasanian court hunted and rested on the way between Ctesiphon and the highlands. The pool is still fed by the spring; the mountain still closes the view.\n\nThe carving began under Ardashir II, who reigned from 379 to 383. His panel, cut straight into the rock face, shows him receiving the ring of kingship from Shapur II while the god Mithra stands behind on a lotus; under their feet lies a defeated Roman, probably the emperor Julian. Next to it Shapur II and Shapur III had a small vaulted iwan cut, with their two figures and an inscription in Pahlavi.\n\nThen, two centuries later, the great iwan — attributed to Khosrow II, who reigned from 590 to 628 and brought the empire to its largest extent and its collapse. It is the most elaborate rock-cut monument the Sasanians left: an arch framed by winged figures and trees of life, a back wall with the king invested by Ahura Mazda and Anahita above a mounted knight in chain mail, and side walls covered, inch by inch, with the royal hunt.",
